## Example

Let $\mathscr{A}$ be the **algebra of finite/cofinite subsets** of $\mathbb{N}$:

$$\mathscr{A} = \{A \subseteq \mathbb{N} : A \text{ is finite, or } \mathbb{N}\setminus A \text{ is finite}\}.$$

This is an algebra (closed under finite unions, complements, contains $\mathbb{N}$), but not a $\sigma$-algebra (e.g. the set of even numbers is neither finite nor cofinite).

Define $\mu : \mathscr{A} \to [0,\infty]$ by

$$\mu(A) = \begin{cases} 0 & \text{if } A \text{ is finite},\\ 1 & \text{if } A \text{ is cofinite}. \end{cases}$$

### $\mu$ is finitely additive

Let $A, B \in \mathscr{A}$ be disjoint.

- If both $A, B$ are finite, then $A \cup B$ is finite, so $\mu(A\cup B) = 0 = 0+0 = \mu(A)+\mu(B)$.
- If one is cofinite, say $A$ is cofinite, then since $A\cup B \supseteq A$ is cofinite we have $\mu(A\cup B)=1$. But $A$ cofinite and $A\cap B = \emptyset$ forces $B \subseteq \mathbb{N}\setminus A$, which is finite, so $B$ is finite and $\mu(B)=0$. Hence $\mu(A)+\mu(B) = 1+0 = 1 = \mu(A\cup B)$.

So $\mu$ is finitely additive. $\mu(\mathbb{N})=1$ and $\mu(\emptyset)=0$.

### $\mu$ is not countably additive

We have $\mathbb{N} = \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} \{n\}$, a disjoint union of sets in $\mathscr{A}$. Each $\{n\}$ is finite, so $\mu(\{n\})=0$, giving $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\mu(\{n\}) = 0$. But $\mu(\mathbb{N}) = 1$. Thus

$$\mu\!\left(\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}\{n\}\right) = 1 \neq 0 = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\mu(\{n\}),$$

so $\mu$ is **not** countably additive.

### $\sigma(\mathscr{A}) = \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$

Every singleton $\{n\}$ is finite, hence in $\mathscr{A}$. Therefore every subset $S\subseteq\mathbb{N}$, being $S = \bigcup_{n\in S}\{n\}$, lies in $\sigma(\mathscr{A})$. So $\sigma(\mathscr{A}) = \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$.

### No countably additive extension to $\sigma(\mathscr{A})$ exists

Suppose, for contradiction, that $\bar\mu : \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}) \to [0,\infty]$ is a countably additive measure extending $\mu$ (i.e. $\bar\mu|_{\mathscr{A}} = \mu$).

Since each singleton $\{n\}\in\mathscr{A}$, we must have $\bar\mu(\{n\}) = \mu(\{n\}) = 0$ for every $n$. By countable additivity,

$$\bar\mu(\mathbb{N}) = \bar\mu\!\left(\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}\{n\}\right) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\bar\mu(\{n\}) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} 0 = 0.$$

But $\mathbb{N}\in\mathscr{A}$ and $\mathbb{N}$ is cofinite, so $\bar\mu(\mathbb{N}) = \mu(\mathbb{N}) = 1$. This gives $0 = 1$, a contradiction.

### Conclusion

The finitely additive (but not countably additive) measure $\mu$ on the algebra $\mathscr{A}$ of finite/cofinite subsets of $\mathbb{N}$ **cannot be extended to any countably additive measure on $\sigma(\mathscr{A}) = \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$**. This demonstrates that finite additivity alone is insufficient to guarantee a countably additive extension to the generated $\sigma$-algebra — exactly the obstruction that the Carathéodory extension theorem circumvents by requiring countable additivity (or at least countable subadditivity) of the premeasure on the algebra.

### PROOF COMPLETE
